  "response_text": "This is a bad idea because always picking the highest-priority task can lead to starvation of lower-priority threads. If there are always higher-priority threads ready to run, the low-priority ones may never get CPU time. This can break essential background functions, cause system instability, or lead to unresponsiveness. In real-world systems, we need fairness\u2014scheduling policies must ensure that all threads get a chance to run, even if their priority is low. This is why systems often use techniques like priority aging or priority-based round-robin instead of strict greedy scheduling.\\n",
